.\" /usr/bin/pbcopy and /usr/bin/pbpaste.
.TH PBCOPY 1 "September 24, 2026" "LibreDarwin"
.SH NAME
\fBpbcopy\fR, \fBpbpaste\fR \- copy data to and from the pasteboard (Clipboard)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pbcopy
[\fB\-help\fR] [\fB\-pboard\fR {\fBgeneral\fR | \fBruler\fR | \fBfind\fR | \fBfont\fR}]
.PP
.B pbpaste
[\fB\-help\fR] [\fB\-pboard\fR {\fBgeneral\fR | \fBruler\fR | \fBfind\fR | \fBfont\fR}]
[\fB\-Prefer\fR {\fBtxt\fR | \fBrtf\fR | \fBps\fR}]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pbcopy
reads standard input and stores it on the named pasteboard.
When no pasteboard is given, the general pasteboard is used.
Input is held as plain text unless it begins with the Encapsulated
PostScript (EPS) file header or the Rich Text Format (RTF) file
header, in which case it is stored under the matching type.
.PP
.B pbpaste
writes the first available pasteboard item to standard output in the
given preference order: an explicit
.B \-Prefer
argument first, then plain text, Encapsulated PostScript, and Rich
Text Format.  If none of those types is present, it produces no
output.
.PP
Both programs take their text encoding from the locale environment
variables, falling back to the C encoding when one cannot be
determined.  For example, setting LANG=en_US.UTF-8 selects UTF-8 for
input and output.  Use of UTF-8 is recommended; Terminal.app sets the
locale it uses accordingly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-pboard \ {general | ruler | find | font}
Select the pasteboard.
.B general
is the default.
.TP
.BI \-Prefer \ {txt | rtf | ps}
Select the type of data
.B pbpaste
looks for first.  Without it the search order is plain text,
Encapsulated PostScript, then Rich Text Format.
The
.B txt
option names a preference for plain text; the deprecated
.B ascii
option is also accepted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR open (1),
.BR textutil (1),
.BR tiffutil (1)
.SH BUGS
There is no way to tell
.B pbpaste
to get only a specified data type.